course: dictionary
lesson: 9094
title: Sound words and manners
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Slurp is a sound word. It is the noise you make when you drink or eat noisily, sucking in air with your food or drink. «Soup is eaten quietly, sipping from the side of the spoon, without slurping.» The word «without» is the clue: quiet sipping is the opposite of slurping, so slurping must be a loud, sucking noise.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Soup is eaten quietly, sipping from the side of the spoon, without making a loud sucking noise.» It still makes sense, so you have found the meaning.
explain: Slurp can also be a noun, a slurp. A slurp is a noisy sucking sound, or one mouthful drunk this way. ««Miàntiáo are noodles; slurping is fine» shows that whether slurping is polite depends on where you are and what you are eating.
fact: Slurp means to drink or eat noisily by sucking in air with the food or drink.
fact: As a noun, a slurp is a noisy sucking sound, or a mouthful drunk this way.
fact: «Miàntiáo are noodles; slurping is fine», so with noodles it is not rude to make a noise.
fact: On birthdays, «long-life noodles» are not bitten short; you slurp them whole, because long noodles mean long life.
fact: Soup is eaten «without slurping», sipping quietly from the side of the spoon.
exercise: «Soup is eaten quietly, sipping from the side of the spoon, without slurping.» What does slurping mean? | *Making a noisy sucking sound while eating or drinking | Eating with a fork | Cooking soup slowly | Sipping quietly from the side of the spoon
exercise: On birthdays you «slurp them whole», so the noodles are not bitten short. What does slurp mean here? | Cut them with scissors | Throw them away | Bite them into little pieces | *Eat them noisily by sucking them in whole
exercise: In «The soup made a loud slurp», what kind of word is slurp? | *A noun, a noisy sucking sound | An adjective, a word that describes | A verb, an action you do | A place where soup is made
test: In «He took a slurp of tea», what does a slurp of tea mean? | A cup used for tea | A tea towel for drying | A cold drink with ice | *A mouthful of tea drunk noisily
test: Why does the writer say «without slurping» for soup? | Because spoons are too big to use | *Because soup is eaten quietly, so there is no noisy sucking | Because soup is always cold | Because soup is always too hot to eat
exam: Lily says slurp means to chew loudly. Is she right? | Yes, slurp means chewing very slowly | *No, slurp means drinking or eating noisily by sucking in air | No, slurp means to whisper | Yes, slurp means chewing with the mouth open
